<!DOCTYPE article PUBLIC "-//NLM//DTD Journal Archiving and Interchange DTD v2.3 20070202//EN" "archivearticle.dtd"><article xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" article-type="research-article"><?properties open_access?><front><journal-meta><journal-id journal-id-type="nlm-ta">Emerg Infect Dis</journal-id><journal-id journal-id-type="pmc">eid</journal-id><journal-title>Emerging Infectious Diseases</journal-title><issn pub-type="ppub">1080-6040</issn><issn pub-type="epub">1080-6059</issn><publisher><publisher-name>Centers for Disease Control</publisher-name></publisher></journal-meta><article-meta><article-id pub-id-type="pmid">9284380</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="pmc">2627640</article-id><article-categories><subj-group subj-group-type="heading"><subject>Research Article</subject></subj-group></article-categories><title-group><article-title>Deer ticks (Ixodes scapularis) and the agents of Lyme disease and human granulocytic ehrlichiosis in a New York City park.</article-title></title-group><contrib-group><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Daniels</surname><given-names>T. J.</given-names></name></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Falco</surname><given-names>R. C.</given-names></name></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Schwartz</surname><given-names>I.</given-names></name></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Varde</surname><given-names>S.</given-names></name></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Robbins</surname><given-names>R. G.</given-names></name></contrib></contrib-group><aff>Fordham University, Armonk, New York, USA.</aff><pub-date pub-type="ppub"><season>Jul-Sep</season><year>1997</year></pub-date><volume>3</volume><issue>3</issue><fpage>353</fpage><lpage>355</lpage><abstract><p>Rodent trapping and drag sampling in Van Cortlandt Park, New York City, yielded all stages of Ixodes scapularis, the deer tick vector of Lyme disease and human granulocytic ehrlichiosis (HGE). Polymerase chain reaction analyses of the ticks showed Borrelia burgdorferi and the Ehrlichia sp. that causes HGE.</p></abstract></article-meta></front></article>